<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Covenant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1638</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Covenant was a declaration signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>'s Greyfriars Kirk from 28 February 1638 and then across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, binding its signers to defend the Reformed religion against the innovations of Charles I, especially his new prayer book and the power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. It united most of the nobility, clergy and burghs against the king, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a> and helped start the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-covenant.jpg" alt="National Covenant" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_National_Covenant,_Huntly_House_Museum,_Edinburgh.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
